        "step_anchor": "step-1",
        "title": "Introduction",
        "key_prerequisites": [],
        "has_content_in_snapshot": true,
        "sections": [
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p>The <strong>power rule</strong> allows us to simplify logarithms of exponentiated expressions. It states that\n<span class=\"math\">[MATH: \\log_{n}\\left( c^{\\color{blue}m} \\right) = {\\color{blue}m}\\log_{n}{c}.]</span></p>",
            "readable_text": "The power rule allows us to simplify logarithms of exponentiated expressions. It states that\n[MATH: \\log_{n}\\left( c^{\\color{blue}m} \\right) = {\\color{blue}m}\\log_{n}{c}.]",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p>In other words, the logarithm has the effect of &quot;bringing down the power.&quot;</p>",
            "readable_text": "In other words, the logarithm has the effect of \"bringing down the power.\"",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p>For example, let&#x27;s use the rule to simplify <span class=\"math\">[MATH: \\log\\left(125\\right)\\mathbin{:}]</span>\n<span class=\"math\">[MATH: \\log (125)|= \\log (5^{3}); = 3\\log (5)]</span></p>",
            "readable_text": "For example, let's use the rule to simplify [MATH: \\log\\left(125\\right)\\mathbin{:}]\n[MATH: \\log (125)|= \\log (5^{3}); = 3\\log (5)]",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p><strong>Note:</strong> To understand why the power rule works, notice that we can reach the same result using the product rule:\n<span class=\"math\">[MATH: \\log (125)|= \\log (5^{3}); = \\log (5 \u22c5 5 \u22c5 5); = \\log (5) + \\log (5) + \\log (5); = 3\\log (5)]</span></p>",
            "readable_text": "Note: To understand why the power rule works, notice that we can reach the same result using the product rule:\n[MATH: \\log (125)|= \\log (5^{3}); = \\log (5 \u22c5 5 \u22c5 5); = \\log (5) + \\log (5) + \\log (5); = 3\\log (5)]",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p>The power rule is just a shortcut for the above procedure.</p>",
            "readable_text": "The power rule is just a shortcut for the above procedure.",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          }
        ],
        "images": [],
        "free_entry_blanks": [],
        "select_lists": [],
        "readable_text": "Content:\nThe power rule allows us to simplify logarithms of exponentiated expressions. It states that\n[MATH: \\log_{n}\\left( c^{\\color{blue}m} \\right) = {\\color{blue}m}\\log_{n}{c}.]\n\nContent:\nIn other words, the logarithm has the effect of \"bringing down the power.\"\n\nContent:\nFor example, let's use the rule to simplify [MATH: \\log\\left(125\\right)\\mathbin{:}]\n[MATH: \\log (125)|= \\log (5^{3}); = 3\\log (5)]\n\nContent:\nNote: To understand why the power rule works, notice that we can reach the same result using the product rule:\n[MATH: \\log (125)|= \\log (5^{3}); = \\log (5 \u22c5 5 \u22c5 5); = \\log (5) + \\log (5) + \\log (5); = 3\\log (5)]\n\nContent:\nThe power rule is just a shortcut for the above procedure."
      },
