    "readable_text": "Item 1 \u2014 Tutorial: Introduction\n\nContent:\nThe power rule allows us to simplify logarithms of exponentiated expressions. It states that\n[MATH: \\log_{n}\\left( c^{\\color{blue}m} \\right) = {\\color{blue}m}\\log_{n}{c}.]\n\nContent:\nIn other words, the logarithm has the effect of \"bringing down the power.\"\n\nContent:\nFor example, let's use the rule to simplify [MATH: \\log\\left(125\\right)\\mathbin{:}]\n[MATH: \\log (125)|= \\log (5^{3}); = 3\\log (5)]\n\nContent:\nNote: To understand why the power rule works, notice that we can reach the same result using the product rule:\n[MATH: \\log (125)|= \\log (5^{3}); = \\log (5 \u22c5 5 \u22c5 5); = \\log (5) + \\log (5) + \\log (5); = 3\\log (5)]\n\nContent:\nThe power rule is just a shortcut for the above procedure.\n\n---\n\nItem 2 \u2014 Example: Simplifying the Logarithm of a Power Function Using the Power Rule\n\nExample Prompt:\nBy expressing [MATH: \\ln\\left(x^2\\right)] in the form [MATH: k\\ln x,] determine the value of [MATH: k.]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nFirst, let's recall the power rule for logarithms:\n\n[MATH: \\log_n(c^m)= m\\log_n\\left( c \\right)]\n\nUsing the power rule for logarithms, we get\n[MATH: \\ln (x^{2})|= 2\\ln x.]\n\nTherefore, [MATH: k =2.]\n\n---\n\nItem 3 \u2014 Question 1\n\nPrompt:\nBy expressing [MATH: \\log (81)] in the form [MATH: k\\log (3),] determine the value of [MATH: k.]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 9]\nb. [MATH: (1)/(2)]\nc. [MATH: 2]\nd. [MATH: (1)/(4)]\ne. [MATH: 4]\n\n---\n\nItem 4 \u2014 Question 2\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: \\log (x^{3}) =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: \u2212 (1)/(3)\\log (3x)]\nb. [MATH: (1)/(3)\\log x]\nc. [MATH: 3\\log x]\nd. [MATH: \\log (3x)]\ne. [MATH: \u2212 3\\log x]\n\n---\n\nItem 5 \u2014 Example: Writing an Expression as the Logarithm of a Power Function Using the Power Rule\n\nExample Prompt:\nBy expressing [MATH: \\dfrac{1}{2}\\log_3{x}] in the form [MATH: \\log_3 \\left(x^k\\right),] determine the value of [MATH: k.]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nFirst, let's recall the power rule for logarithms:\n\n[MATH: \\log_n(c^m)= m\\log_n\\left( c \\right)]\n\nUsing the power rule for logarithms, we get\n\n[MATH: (1)/(2)\\log_{3} x|= \\log_{3} (x^{1/2}).]\n\nTherefore, [MATH: k = \\dfrac{1}{2}.]\n\n---\n\nItem 6 \u2014 Question 3\n\nPrompt:\nBy expressing [MATH: 3\\ln (4)] in the form [MATH: \\ln (N),] determine the value of [MATH: N.]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 256]\nb. [MATH: 64]\nc. [MATH: 12]\nd. [MATH: 48]\ne. [MATH: 96]\n\n---\n\nItem 7 \u2014 Question 4\n\nPrompt:\nBy expressing [MATH: (5)/(2)\\log_{2} t] in the form [MATH: \\log_{2} (t^{k}),] determine the value of [MATH: k.]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: (5)/(2)]\nb. [MATH: \u2212 (2)/(5)]\nc. [MATH: (5)/(4)]\nd. [MATH: (2)/(5)]\ne. [MATH: \u2212 (5)/(2)]\n\n---\n\nItem 8 \u2014 Example: Evaluating an Expression Using the Power Rule\n\nExample Prompt:\nFind the value of [MATH: \\ln\\left(x^3\\right)] if [MATH: \\ln(x) = 0.2.]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nFirst, let's recall the power rule for natural logarithms:\n[MATH: \\ln\\left( c^m \\right) = m\\ln{c}]\n\nUsing the power rule for logarithms and the information given, we get\n\n[MATH: \\ln (x^{3})|= 3 \u22c5 \\ln (x); = 3 \u22c5 0.2; = 0.6.]\n\n---\n\nItem 9 \u2014 Question 5\n\nPrompt:\nFind the value of [MATH: \\log_{z} ((1)/(4))] if [MATH: \\log_{z} (4) = 0.6.]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: \u2212 1.2]\nb. [MATH: \u2212 0.6]\nc. [MATH: 0.6]\nd. [MATH: 0.3]\ne. [MATH: \u2212 1.8]\n\n---\n\nItem 10 \u2014 Question 6\n\nPrompt:\nIf [MATH: \\log (x) = 3,] what is [MATH: \\log (x^{3})?]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 18]\nb. [MATH: 81]\nc. [MATH: 6]\nd. [MATH: 27]\ne. [MATH: 9]"
